/**
 * src/build/regr_group.tks   (namespace `teko::build`)
 *
 * GROUPED REGRESSION BUILDS — one build per PROJECT-and-configuration, not one per scenario (owner
 * ruling 2026-07-25): *"Pq um build por cenário se todos os cenários em um tkr (ou mais) estão
 * dentro de um único projeto (tkp)? … poderia executar estes builds ANTES de qualquer teste começar
 * … cada regressão deve pertencer a um único tkp, mas um tkp pode ter vários tkr. Logo, se tenho 10
 * tkr em um único tkp, somente um build é necessário."*
 *
 * WHAT THE RULING COSTS TO HONOUR. A `Given source inline` scenario is not a file in a project — it
 * is a program the runner SYNTHESIZES a scratch project around (`compile_snippet_text`). Two hundred
 * of those synthesized projects are two hundred front-end start-ups and two hundred `cc` runs for
 * programs whose largest is nine lines: on the .31 corpus that is 70% of the whole regression
 * phase's wall time. Folding them into ONE project is therefore the ruling's whole point, and the
 * fold has exactly three obligations, none of them negotiable:
 *
 *   1. every scenario still COMPILES for real, RUNS for real, and has its exit/stdout/stderr checked
 *      for real — the fold may not weaken a single assertion;
 *   2. two snippets that declare the same bare `Point` may not collide — each snippet gets its OWN
 *      NAMESPACE (`src/m<k>/body.tks` → `<project>::m<k>`, the directory-is-namespace rule of
 *      `discover`), which is the language's own answer to that collision (`di_same_name_cross_ns`
 *      proves it end-to-end for classes, interfaces and DI providers alike);
 *   3. a scenario whose BUILD CONFIGURATION differs (`Given backend`, `Given target`, `Given env`)
 *      may not share a build with one that does not — the group key IS the child env plus the
 *      target, so a differing configuration is a different group by construction.
 *
 * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT GROUPED, and why (each is measured, not assumed — see the report in the
 * lane's own notes):
 *
 *   - a `compilation fails` scenario. Its build MUST fail; a group containing it could never build,
 *     so it keeps one build each. This is inevitable, not a gap.
 *   - a `Then object well-formed` / `Then module valid` scenario. The leaf tool inspects the emitted
 *     `<binary>.o` / `.wasm` — in a group that artifact is the WHOLE group's, so the check would no
 *     longer be about the scenario that declared it. Grouping it would silently change what is
 *     checked, which is worse than the build it saves.
 *   - a scenario the host cannot build at all (`target_toolchain_skip_reason`) — it is honest-skipped
 *     BEFORE any build, so it has no build to save.
 *   - a project-based (`.tkp`-on-disk) scenario — those already share one build per `.tkr` file
 *     (`RegrBuilt`), which is what the ruling asks for, already true since 0.3.0.30.
 *   - a snippet whose top-level statements contain a `return` (`snippet_stmts_have_return`) — in the
 *     virtual main a top-level `return e` sets the EXIT CODE, and inside the `entry()` function the
 *     fold moves those statements into it would return a value from a `void` function. One scenario
 *     of the .31 corpus.
 *   - a scenario built by the OWN (native) backend — `REGR_GROUP_UNGROUPABLE_BACKEND_ENV`.
 *
 * WHY A CONFIGURATION CAN STILL NEED SEVERAL BUILDS (`regr_group_for_row`). Namespaces answer the
 * bare-name collision for everything the language resolves canonically — the `di_same_name_cross_ns`
 * regressor proves it for classes, interfaces, methods and DI providers alike. They do NOT answer it
 * everywhere, and folding the .31 corpus found three passes where they do not, each REPRODUCED down
 * to a two-module project:
 *
 *   - the STRUCTURAL-TRAIT SYNTHESIZER (`src/checker/synth.tks`) resolves a derived type's field
 *     types by BARE name. `type Inner = struct Eq & Ord & …` folded beside an unrelated `type Inner
 *     = struct { f: Op | null }` from another namespace makes the `Eq` synthesis walk the WRONG
 *     `Inner` and stop with "structural trait `Eq` cannot derive over field `f`";
 *   - a top-level `const` resolves by bare name: `const B: byte = 5` folded beside `const B: i64 =
 *     A + 1` makes `B to i64` read as a no-op and earns the cast-hygiene D1 rejection;
 *   - a top-level `fn` PASSED AS A VALUE resolves by bare name: `fold(…, add)` folded beside three
 *     other members' `add` picks one of theirs and fails on the argument type.
 *
 * All three are the defect class issues #284/#290 fixed for DI, still open elsewhere — COMPILER bugs,
 * reported up, not worked around in the compiler from here. What this module does instead is refuse
 * to CREATE the collision: a configuration's rows are packed into as many groups as it takes for no
 * two members of one group to declare the same bare TOP-LEVEL name. The packing is greedy and
 * first-fit, and on the .31 corpus it turns one 203-row configuration into eleven builds instead of
 * 203 — while making the fold immune to any other bare-name-keyed pass, not just these three.
 *
 * THE FALLBACK IS THE SAFETY PROOF. A grouped build is a pure OPTIMISATION: when it succeeds, its
 * members run from the group binary; when it FAILS for any reason at all, every member silently
 * reverts to its own scratch-project build (`tkr_ensure_artifact`, the pre-grouping path) and the
 * gate is byte-for-byte what it was before. That is what makes the tail classification below safe to
 * be a heuristic: BOTH ways of getting it wrong are compile errors, never wrong answers, and a
 * compile error costs only the speed-up.
 */

/**
 * REGR_GROUP_PROJECT_NAME — the canonical root name every generated group project is stamped with.
 * Fixed rather than derived, so the built binary's path (`<dir>/bin/<name>`) is known to the runner
 * without re-parsing the synthesized `.tkp` — the same reason `SNIPPET_PROJECT_NAME` is fixed.
 */
const REGR_GROUP_PROJECT_NAME: str = "regrgroup"

/**
 * REGR_GROUP_ENTRY_VAR — the environment-variable key the group binary reads to learn WHICH member
 * scenario to run. An environment variable and NOT an argv slot: a scenario's `Given args` are its
 * own fold-opaque operands, and shifting them by one to make room for a selector would change what
 * every `args`-reading fixture observes.
 */
const REGR_GROUP_ENTRY_VAR: str = "TEKO_REGR_ENTRY"

/**
 * REGR_GROUP_UNKNOWN_ENTRY_EXIT — the exit status the group binary's dispatcher takes when the
 * selector names no member. It cannot happen in a correct run (the runner sets the selector from the
 * very plan that generated the dispatcher), so its whole job is to be LOUD if it ever does: the row
 * fails with this code against whatever it expected, and the dispatcher names the missing entry on
 * stderr. Silence here would let a mis-mapped scenario report the previous member's result.
 */
const REGR_GROUP_UNKNOWN_ENTRY_EXIT: i32 = 97

/**
 * REGR_GROUP_MIN_MEMBERS — how many rows a group must hold before it is worth generating. A group of
 * ONE is the per-scenario build it replaces, plus a generated project and a dispatcher: strictly
 * more machinery for exactly the same build count. Below this bound the rows stay on the ordinary
 * per-scenario path.
 */
const REGR_GROUP_MIN_MEMBERS: u64 = 2

/**
 * REGR_GROUP_UNGROUPABLE_BACKEND_ENV — the child-env token whose presence makes a row ungroupable:
 * the OWN (native) backend selector that `Given backend = "own"` lowers to (`tkr_backend_env`).
 *
 * A group needs a DISPATCHER, and a dispatcher needs to read a selector and compare it — and the
 * own backend can lower none of the three ways to do that today. Measured, not assumed, on the .31
 * corpus: `teko::env::var` returns `str | error` and the backend stops with "unknown variant case
 * `str`"; `teko::env::args` stops with "fat-pointer receiver `call` not yet lowered (N2)"; a `match`
 * over the union stops with "fat-pointer receiver `match-expression` not yet lowered (N2)". The
 * fifteen own-backend rows are exactly the tiny arithmetic programs that stay inside what the
 * backend can lower, which is why they pass one build at a time and could not survive a dispatcher.
 *
 * This is a capability gate, not a policy: DELETE this constant and its test in
 * `regr_row_groupable` the day the native backend lowers a string compare plus either env or argv,
 * and those rows fold like every other. Until then the honest thing is to leave them their own
 * builds instead of failing a group build on every run to rediscover it.
 */
const REGR_GROUP_UNGROUPABLE_BACKEND_ENV: str = "TEKO_BACKEND=native"

/**
 * REGR_GROUP_KEY_SEP — the separator joining a group key's parts (the target and each env token).
 * A newline, because an env token is `K=V` and a target is an os-arch name — neither may contain
 * one, so no two distinct configurations can ever collide into one key.
 */
const REGR_GROUP_KEY_SEP: str = "\n"

/**
 * RegrGroupSpec — the BUILD CONFIGURATION shared by every member of one group: the per-scenario
 * child env (`Given env`/`Given backend`/`Given target` already lowered to `K=V` tokens) and the
 * declared target. Two rows may share a build exactly when both match.
 */
pub type RegrGroupSpec = struct {
    /** the child env every member of this group compiles under (`K=V` tokens). */
    env: []str
    /** the declared os-arch target ("" / `HOST_TARGET` → the build host's own). */
    target: str
}

/**
 * RegrGroupMember — the binding of ONE scenario row to its slot inside a grouped build: the row's
 * scratch-prefix `key` (the runner computes the identical string for the same row when it runs it —
 * see `regr_group_slot`), the `group` it belongs to, the `module` namespace its snippet was written
 * into, and the snippet `source` itself.
 */
pub type RegrGroupMember = struct {
    /** the row's scratch-file prefix — its identity, shared by the plan pass and the run pass. */
    key: str
    /** the index of the group this row belongs to (into `RegrGroupPlan.specs`). */
    group: u64
    /** the namespace segment this row's snippet was written into (`m<k>`). */
    module: str
    /**
     * the snippet source this row CONTRIBUTES to its group — or "" when an earlier member of the
     * same group already contributed the byte-identical source and this row simply reuses its
     * module. The reuse is what keeps a `Scenario Outline` whose placeholders appear only in
     * `args`/`Then` (its fifteen rows share one program) from claiming fifteen modules and
     * colliding with itself fifteen ways.
     */
    source: str
}

/**
 * RegrGroupPlan — the whole plan: one entry per GROUP (a build configuration plus a name-collision-
 * free set of members, so one configuration may appear in several entries), and every row assigned
 * to one of them.
 */
pub type RegrGroupPlan = struct {
    /** each group's build configuration, in first-appearance order (a configuration may repeat). */
    specs: []RegrGroupSpec
    /** every groupable row, in walk order. */
    members: []RegrGroupMember
}

/**
 * RegrGroupBuild — the outcome of building ONE group: whether it built (`ok`), the artifact its
 * members run, and what the build cost. A group that did NOT build is not an error — its members
 * fall back to their own per-scenario builds — so `ok = false` carries no verdict, only a lost
 * speed-up.
 */
pub type RegrGroupBuild = struct {
    /** true when the group project compiled and its members may run from `binp`. */
    ok: bool
    /** the built artifact every member runs (target-resolved — a wasm group yields the module). */
    binp: str
    /** how many rows this group covers (the report's "scenarios" column). */
    members: u64
    /** the build's own three-phase measurement (one build, whatever it cost). */
    times: PhaseTimes
}

/**
 * RegrGroups — the resolved grouping the runner consults per row: the member bindings and the
 * per-group build outcomes, together. Empty (`members = []`) when nothing was grouped, in which case
 * every lookup misses and the runner behaves exactly as it did before grouping existed.
 */
pub type RegrGroups = struct {
    /** every groupable row's binding. */
    members: []RegrGroupMember
    /** the per-group build outcomes, indexed by `RegrGroupMember.group`. */
    builds: []RegrGroupBuild
}

/**
 * RegrGroupSlot — what a row needs to run from a grouped build instead of building its own: the
 * artifact, and the selector naming its own entry inside it.
 */
pub type RegrGroupSlot = struct {
    /** the group artifact this row runs. */
    binp: str
    /** the value of `REGR_GROUP_ENTRY_VAR` that selects this row's snippet. */
    module: str
}

/**
 * regr_groups_empty — the grouping that groups nothing. The identity the runner uses when planning
 * found no eligible row, so the "is this row grouped?" question has exactly one answer path.
 *
 * @return an empty grouping (every lookup misses)
 */
pub fn regr_groups_empty() -> RegrGroups {
    RegrGroups { members = teko::list::empty(); builds = teko::list::empty() }
}

/**
 * regr_group_key — the key two rows must share to share one build: the declared target followed by
 * every child-env token, joined by `REGR_GROUP_KEY_SEP`. Keying on the WHOLE env (rather than on the
 * `TEKO_BACKEND`/`TEKO_TARGET` tokens the runner happens to know about) is the conservative choice
 * and the honest one: an arbitrary `Given env` token may or may not reach the compiler, and a key
 * that guessed wrong would silently share a build between two differently-configured scenarios.
 *
 * @param t  the lowered row
 * @return   the row's build-configuration key
 */
fn regr_group_key(t: Tkr) -> str {
    mut key = t.target
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= t.env.len { break }
        key = key ~ REGR_GROUP_KEY_SEP ~ t.env[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    key
}

/**
 * snippet_tail_is_value — does a snippet's LAST top-level statement supply the program's EXIT CODE?
 *
 * The virtual-main rule (#423/#382, `lower_main_tail_stmt`) is that the last loose top-level
 * statement, when it is a NON-VOID expression, IS the exit code — so `total()` at the end of a
 * snippet means "exit with what `total()` returned", while `exit(0)` or `if bad() != 0 { exit(1) }`
 * are void statements that fall through to exit 0. A grouped snippet's statements move into a
 * function body, where that rule does not apply, so the value tail must be made explicit
 * (`exit(<tail>)`) and the void tail must be left alone.
 *
 * This is a SYNTACTIC classification, and it is allowed to be one because both ways of being wrong
 * are COMPILE ERRORS, never wrong answers: a value tail left bare in a `void` function is rejected
 * ("the function's final expression does not match its declared return type"), and a void tail
 * wrapped in `exit(…)` is rejected for passing `void` to it. Either rejection fails the GROUP build,
 * which drops every member back to its own per-scenario build — the pre-grouping behaviour, green
 * and merely slower.
 *
 * @param tail  the snippet's last top-level statement chunk
 * @return      true when the chunk is an expression whose value is the exit code
 */
fn snippet_tail_is_value(tail: str) -> bool {
    let t = tkr_trim(tail)
    if t.len == 0 { return false }
    if mf_starts_with(t, "exit(") || mf_starts_with(t, "panic(") { return false }
    if mf_starts_with(t, "let ") || mf_starts_with(t, "mut ") { return false }
    if mf_starts_with(t, "if ") || mf_starts_with(t, "match ") { return false }
    if mf_starts_with(t, "loop") || mf_starts_with(t, "for ") || mf_starts_with(t, "while ") { return false }
    if mf_starts_with(t, "defer") || mf_starts_with(t, "adopt") || mf_starts_with(t, "#") { return false }
    !snippet_tail_is_assignment(t)
}

/**
 * snippet_tail_is_assignment — is `t` an ASSIGNMENT statement (`x = e`, `p.f = e`, `xs[i] = e`, or a
 * compound `+=`-family form)? An assignment is void, so it must not be wrapped in `exit(…)` — and it
 * is the one void shape that opens with an ordinary expression instead of a keyword, so
 * `snippet_tail_is_value`'s keyword tests cannot see it.
 *
 * Detects the FIRST line's first `=` at bracket depth 0 that is not part of a comparison (`==`,
 * `!=`, `<=`, `>=`) nor of a match arrow (`=>`), skipping string/char literals. Depth 0 is what
 * makes `exit(x = 1)`-shaped false positives impossible and what keeps a keyword-argument-looking
 * `f(a = 1)` (were the language to grow one) out of the answer.
 *
 * @param t  the trimmed tail chunk
 * @return   true when the chunk's first line is an assignment
 */
fn snippet_tail_is_assignment(t: str) -> bool {
    mut depth: i64 = 0
    mut open = false
    mut quote: byte = b'"'
    mut k: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if k >= t.len { break }
        let c = t[k]
        if c == b'\n' { break }
        if open {
            if c == b'\\' { k = k + 2; continue }
            if c == quote { open = false }
            k = k + 1
            continue
        }
        if c == b'"' || c == b'\'' { open = true; quote = c; k = k + 1; continue }
        if c == b'(' || c == b'[' || c == b'{' { depth = depth + 1; k = k + 1; continue }
        if c == b')' || c == b']' || c == b'}' { depth = depth - 1; k = k + 1; continue }
        if c == b'=' && depth == 0 {
            let prev = if k > 0 { t[k - 1] } else { b' ' }
            let next = if k + 1 < t.len { t[k + 1] } else { b' ' }
            let is_cmp = prev == b'=' || prev == b'!' || prev == b'<' || prev == b'>' || next == b'='
            let is_arrow = next == b'>'
            if !is_cmp && !is_arrow { return true }
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    false
}

/**
 * snippet_stmts_have_return — does the snippet's STATEMENT half contain a `return` keyword anywhere?
 * Such a snippet is refused by the fold: in the virtual main a top-level `return e` SETS THE EXIT
 * CODE, while inside the `void entry()` the fold moves those statements into, the very same `return
 * e` returns a value from a void function and is rejected. Rewriting it would mean deciding, per
 * `return`, whether it exits the program or a nested lambda — a judgement this module has no way to
 * make from source text, and one build is not worth guessing at.
 *
 * The scan is a WORD scan outside string/char literals, so `"return"` inside a literal never counts.
 * A `return` inside a COMMENT does count — that only ever costs the snippet its fold, never a wrong
 * answer, and the cheap conservative reading is the right trade here.
 *
 * @param stmts  the snippet's statement half (`SnippetSplit.stmts`)
 * @return       true when a `return` keyword appears in it
 */
fn snippet_stmts_have_return(stmts: str) -> bool {
    mut open = false
    mut quote: byte = b'"'
    mut k: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if k >= stmts.len { break }
        let c = stmts[k]
        if open {
            if c == b'\\' { k = k + 2; continue }
            if c == quote { open = false }
            k = k + 1
            continue
        }
        if c == b'"' || c == b'\'' { open = true; quote = c; k = k + 1; continue }
        if c == b'r' && snippet_word_at(stmts, k, "return") { return true }
        k = k + 1
    }
    false
}

/**
 * snippet_word_at — does `word` occur in `s` at `at` as a WHOLE identifier — neither preceded nor
 * followed by an identifier byte? The keyword test behind `snippet_stmts_have_return`, so
 * `returned_value` is not mistaken for a `return`.
 *
 * @param s     the text
 * @param at    the candidate start offset
 * @param word  the keyword to match
 * @return      true when `word` sits at `at` on identifier boundaries
 */
fn snippet_word_at(s: str, at: u64, word: str) -> bool {
    if at + word.len > s.len { return false }
    if teko::str::slice(s, at, at + word.len) != word { return false }
    if at > 0 && snippet_is_ident_byte(s[at - 1]) { return false }
    let after = at + word.len
    if after < s.len && snippet_is_ident_byte(s[after]) { return false }
    true
}

/**
 * snippet_is_ident_byte — is `c` a byte an identifier may contain (letter, digit, `_`)? The
 * word-boundary predicate.
 *
 * @param c  the byte
 * @return   true for a letter, a digit or `_`
 */
fn snippet_is_ident_byte(c: byte) -> bool {
    is_ascii_alpha(c) || (c >= b'0' && c <= b'9') || c == b'_'
}

/**
 * regr_env_has — does `env` carry the exact `K=V` token `tok`?
 *
 * @param env  the row's child env
 * @param tok  the token to look for
 * @return     true when present
 */
fn regr_env_has(env: []str, tok: str) -> bool {
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= env.len { break }
        if env[i] == tok { return true }
        i = i + 1
    }
    false
}

/**
 * regr_group_module_text — the generated module file for ONE member snippet: its top-level
 * DECLARATIONS verbatim, then its top-level STATEMENTS wrapped in `pub fn entry()`.
 *
 * Declarations and statements land in the SAME file (and therefore the same namespace), which is
 * strictly better than the ungrouped shape's `main.tks` + `src/regr_decls.tks` split — a snippet's
 * statements go on seeing its own declarations unqualified, while another member's same-named
 * declaration is a different namespace entirely.
 *
 * @param src_text  the member's inline snippet source
 * @return          the module file's whole text
 */
fn regr_group_module_text(src_text: str) -> str {
    let split = split_snippet_decls_and_stmts(src_text)
    let head = teko::str::slice(split.stmts, 0, split.stmts.len - split.tail.len)
    let tail = tkr_trim(split.tail)
    let body = if snippet_tail_is_value(tail) { head ~ "exit(" ~ tail ~ ")\n" } else { head ~ tail ~ "\n" }
    split.decls ~ "\n/** The scenario body this group member runs. */\npub fn entry() {\n" ~ body ~ "}\n"
}

/**
 * regr_group_manifest_text — the generated `.tkp` of a group project: a `binary` artifact named
 * `REGR_GROUP_PROJECT_NAME` rooted at `src`, so every `src/m<k>/` directory becomes its own
 * namespace (`discover`'s directory rule) and the generated `main.tks` at the project root stays the
 * virtual-main entry.
 *
 * @return the `.tkp` text
 */
fn regr_group_manifest_text() -> str {
    "name = \"" ~ REGR_GROUP_PROJECT_NAME ~ "\"\nsource = \"src\"\n\n[artifact]\nkind = \"binary\"\n"
}

/**
 * regr_group_dispatch_line — the generated `main.tks` line that routes the selector to ONE member's
 * `entry()`. `hit` is set BEFORE the call so an entry that DIVERGES (`exit(…)`, the common case)
 * and one that FALLS THROUGH are handled by the same line.
 *
 * @param module  the member's namespace segment
 * @return        the dispatcher line
 */
fn regr_group_dispatch_line(module: str) -> str {
    $"if regr_entry == \"{module}\" {{ regr_hit = true; {module}::entry() }}\n"
}

/**
 * regr_group_main_text — the generated `main.tks` of a group project: read the selector, run the
 * member it names, and end.
 *
 * The two exits are the whole contract. Falling off the end of the selected `entry()` is a snippet
 * whose own virtual main fell through, which exits 0 — so the dispatcher's last statement is
 * `exit(0)`. A selector naming NO member cannot happen (the runner sets it from this very plan) and
 * therefore must be loud if it ever does: it names the missing entry on stderr and takes
 * `REGR_GROUP_UNKNOWN_ENTRY_EXIT`, which the row then reports as an exit mismatch.
 *
 * The dispatcher deliberately contains no unreachable statement: from the compiler's view every
 * `entry()` may return, so the trailing `exit(0)` is a reachable end, not dead code.
 *
 * @param dispatch  the concatenated per-member dispatcher lines
 * @return          the `main.tks` text
 */
fn regr_group_main_text(dispatch: str) -> str {
    mut out = $"let regr_entry = match teko::env::var(\"{REGR_GROUP_ENTRY_VAR}\") {{ str as v => v; error => \"\" }}\n"
    out = out ~ "mut regr_hit = false\n"
    out = out ~ dispatch
    out = out ~ "if !regr_hit {\n"
    out = out ~ "    teko::io::eprintln(teko::str::concat(\"teko: regression group: no member entry named \", regr_entry))\n"
    out = out ~ $"    exit({REGR_GROUP_UNKNOWN_ENTRY_EXIT})\n"
    out = out ~ "}\n"
    out = out ~ "exit(0)\n"
    out
}

/**
 * regr_row_source — the SNIPPET TEXT a row contributes to a group, whichever way the row spells it:
 * the `Given source inline` body, or the contents of the `Given source = "<file>"` file resolved
 * against `regr_dir`. "" when the row carries neither (a project-based row) or when the named file
 * cannot be read.
 *
 * A file-based row folds exactly like an inline one, and it HAS to: since the owner banned inline
 * code in a `.tkr` ("está proibido inline code em tkr"), a `.tkr` can only name its snippet as a
 * file — so a fold that only understood `source_inline` would have quietly charged one build per
 * scenario for the whole corpus, which is the cost the fold exists to remove. Measured on the .31
 * corpus at the moment the ban landed: the F5 backend-differential rows alone went from 2 grouped
 * builds to 30 individual ones until this function existed.
 *
 * An unreadable file is NOT an error here: the ordinary run reports a missing `Given source` as the
 * manifest error it is, and planning must never be the thing that reports it (the same rule
 * `regr_plan_file` follows for an unparseable `.tkr`).
 *
 * @param regr_dir  the directory the row's `.tkr` lives in (a `Given source` is relative to it)
 * @param t         the lowered row
 * @return          the snippet text, or "" when the row has none
 */
fn regr_row_source(regr_dir: str, t: Tkr) -> str {
    if t.source_inline.len > 0 { return t.source_inline }
    if t.source_file.len == 0 { return "" }
    match teko::io::read_file(regr_dir ~ "/" ~ t.source_file) { str as s => s; error => "" }
}

/**
 * regr_row_groupable — may this row share a build with the other rows of its configuration? Every
 * exclusion is stated (and argued) in this file's header; the order here is cheapest-test-first.
 *
 * @param regr_dir  the directory the row's `.tkr` lives in (to resolve a `Given source` file)
 * @param t         the lowered row
 * @return          true when the row may be folded into a group
 */
fn regr_row_groupable(regr_dir: str, t: Tkr) -> bool {
    if t.kind == TkrKind::CompileFail { return false }
    if t.dep_dir.len > 0 || t.in_place { return false }
    if t.check_wellformed || t.check_module_valid { return false }
    if regr_env_has(t.env, REGR_GROUP_UNGROUPABLE_BACKEND_ENV) { return false }
    if target_toolchain_skip_reason(t.target).len > 0 { return false }
    let src = regr_row_source(regr_dir, t)
    if src.len == 0 { return false }
    !snippet_stmts_have_return(split_snippet_decls_and_stmts(src).stmts)
}

/**
 * snippet_head_ident_after — the identifier that follows `kw` at the head of `head` (a chunk's text
 * with its leading modifier tokens already stripped), or "" when `head` does not open with `kw`.
 * The name-claim reader: `type Point = struct …` yields `Point`.
 *
 * @param head  the declaration head, past any `pub`/`exp`/`unsafe`/`extern` modifier
 * @param kw    the declaration keyword to match, trailing space included (e.g. `"type "`)
 * @return      the declared name, or "" when `head` does not start with `kw`
 */
fn snippet_head_ident_after(head: str, kw: str) -> str {
    if !mf_starts_with(head, kw) { return "" }
    mut k = kw.len
    loop {
        if k >= head.len { break }
        if head[k] != b' ' && head[k] != b'\t' { break }
        k = k + 1
    }
    mut e = k
    loop {
        if e >= head.len { break }
        if !snippet_is_ident_byte(head[e]) { break }
        e = e + 1
    }
    teko::str::slice(head, k, e)
}

/**
 * snippet_line_declared_name — the bare name `line` declares at TOP LEVEL (`type`/`class`/
 * `interface`/`flags`/`const`/`fn`, after any modifier tokens), or "" when it declares none.
 *
 * All six kinds share ONE claim namespace: a `type Foo` and a `fn Foo` in two different members
 * would be claimed against each other, which over-claims by a group at worst and never lets a real
 * collision through. Under-claiming is the expensive mistake here, not over-claiming.
 *
 * @param line  a top-level declaration line of a snippet's declaration half
 * @return      the declared bare name, or ""
 */
fn snippet_line_declared_name(line: str) -> str {
    let t = tkr_trim(line)
    let head = teko::str::slice(t, snippet_chunk_head_keyword_start(t), t.len)
    let as_type = snippet_head_ident_after(head, "type ")
    if as_type.len > 0 { return as_type }
    let as_class = snippet_head_ident_after(head, "class ")
    if as_class.len > 0 { return as_class }
    let as_iface = snippet_head_ident_after(head, "interface ")
    if as_iface.len > 0 { return as_iface }
    let as_flags = snippet_head_ident_after(head, "flags ")
    if as_flags.len > 0 { return as_flags }
    let as_const = snippet_head_ident_after(head, "const ")
    if as_const.len > 0 { return as_const }
    snippet_head_ident_after(head, "fn ")
}

/**
 * snippet_declared_names — every bare name a snippet's declaration half introduces AT TOP LEVEL.
 * The claim set a group member holds, so no other member of the same group may declare any of them.
 *
 * TOP LEVEL is the whole point of the brace-depth walk here, and it is not a nicety: a class's own
 * METHODS are addressed through their type (`Dog::make`), so forty-seven members each declaring a
 * `pub fn make()` inside their own class collide with nothing — claiming those would have forced
 * forty-seven groups for no reason at all. Only a declaration that begins a chunk at depth 0 is
 * claimed; the walk mirrors `split_snippet_decls_and_stmts` (the same literal-and-comment-aware
 * `snippet_brace_delta`, the same doc-comment skip, the same "an attribute line accumulates onto
 * the declaration it decorates" rule).
 *
 * @param decls  the snippet's declaration half (`SnippetSplit.decls`)
 * @return       the declared bare names, in source order (duplicates harmless)
 */
fn snippet_declared_names(decls: str) -> []str {
    mut out: []str = teko::list::empty()
    mut depth: i64 = 0
    mut in_chunk = false
    mut in_str = false
    mut in_comment = false
    mut cur: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if cur >= decls.len { break }
        mut eol = cur
        loop {
            if eol >= decls.len { break }
            if decls[eol] == b'\n' { break }
            eol = eol + 1
        }
        let line = teko::str::slice(decls, cur, eol)
        let next = if eol < decls.len { eol + 1 } else { eol }
        let trimmed = tkr_trim(line)
        if !in_chunk && trimmed.len == 0 { cur = next; continue }
        if !in_chunk && mf_starts_with(trimmed, "/**") { cur = snippet_skip_doc_comment(decls, cur); continue }
        if !in_chunk && !mf_starts_with(trimmed, "#") {
            let name = snippet_line_declared_name(trimmed)
            if name.len > 0 { out = teko::list::push(out, name) }
            in_chunk = true
        }
        let scan = snippet_brace_delta(line, in_str, in_comment)
        depth = depth + scan.delta
        in_str = scan.in_str
        in_comment = scan.in_comment
        if depth <= 0 { depth = 0; in_chunk = false }
        cur = next
    }
    out
}

/**
 * RegrNameClaim — one bare TOP-LEVEL name a group already holds. The packing constraint made explicit:
 * a flat claim list (rather than a per-group name slice) because a slice-of-slices is not an
 * emittable struct field, and a linear scan over a few hundred claims costs nothing next to a build.
 */
type RegrNameClaim = struct {
    /** the group holding the claim. */
    group: u64
    /** the bare top-level name it holds. */
    name: str
}

/**
 * regr_claims_conflict — would adding a member declaring `names` to group `g` create a bare-name
 * collision with what `g` already holds?
 *
 * @param claims  every claim made so far
 * @param g       the candidate group
 * @param names   the member's declared bare top-level names
 * @return        true when the group already holds one of them
 */
fn regr_claims_conflict(claims: []RegrNameClaim, g: u64, names: []str) -> bool {
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= claims.len { break }
        let c = claims[i]
        if c.group == g {
            mut j: u64 = 0
            loop {
                if j >= names.len { break }
                if names[j] == c.name { return true }
                j = j + 1
            }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    false
}

/**
 * regr_claims_add — `claims` with every name in `names` claimed for group `g`.
 *
 * @param claims  the claims so far
 * @param g       the group taking the names
 * @param names   the names to claim
 * @return        the extended claim list
 */
fn regr_claims_add(claims: []RegrNameClaim, g: u64, names: []str) -> []RegrNameClaim {
    mut out = claims
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= names.len { break }
        out = teko::list::push(out, RegrNameClaim { group = g; name = names[i] })
        i = i + 1
    }
    out
}

/**
 * RegrPlanAcc — the plan under construction: the group keys seen so far (parallel to `specs` — and
 * NOT distinct, since one configuration may need several groups), the configurations they stand for,
 * the bare-name claims each group holds, the member bindings, and the running module counter. One
 * struct so the per-file/per-feature/per-row walk threads a single value instead of five.
 */
type RegrPlanAcc = struct {
    /** each group's configuration key (repeats when a configuration needed several groups). */
    keys: []str
    /** the configuration behind each group (parallel to `keys`). */
    specs: []RegrGroupSpec
    /** the bare top-level names each group holds. */
    claims: []RegrNameClaim
    /** every groupable row's binding, in walk order. */
    members: []RegrGroupMember
    /** how many members have been assigned a module name (the `m<k>` counter). */
    next_module: u64
}

/**
 * regr_plan_empty — the empty plan accumulator.
 *
 * @return an accumulator with no groups, no members and a zeroed module counter
 */
fn regr_plan_empty() -> RegrPlanAcc {
    RegrPlanAcc { keys = teko::list::empty(); specs = teko::list::empty(); claims = teko::list::empty(); members = teko::list::empty(); next_module = 0 }
}

/**
 * regr_group_for_row — the FIRST group that both matches the row's build configuration and can take
 * its declared top-level names without a bare-name collision, or `acc.keys.len` when a new group is
 * needed. First-fit rather than best-fit: the corpus's collisions cluster on a handful of names
 * (`Point`, `Dog`, `Animal`, …), so first-fit already packs the bulk into one group, and a smarter
 * packing would trade a real complexity cost for at most a build or two.
 *
 * @param acc    the accumulator
 * @param gkey   the row's build-configuration key
 * @param names  the row's declared bare top-level names
 * @return       the group to place the row in, or `acc.keys.len` for a fresh one
 */
fn regr_group_for_row(acc: RegrPlanAcc, gkey: str, names: []str) -> u64 {
    mut g: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if g >= acc.keys.len { break }
        if acc.keys[g] == gkey && !regr_claims_conflict(acc.claims, g, names) { return g }
        g = g + 1
    }
    acc.keys.len
}

/**
 * regr_plan_add_row — fold ONE groupable row into the accumulator: place it in the first group that
 * matches its configuration and can hold its top-level names (opening a new one when none can), claim
 * those names for that group, and give the row a fresh module name.
 *
 * @param acc  the accumulator
 * @param t    the lowered row
 * @param key  the row's scratch-file prefix (its identity across the plan and run passes)
 * @param src  the row's resolved snippet text (`regr_row_source` — inline body or named file)
 * @return     the accumulator with the row bound
 */
fn regr_plan_add_row(acc: RegrPlanAcc, t: Tkr, key: str, src: str) -> RegrPlanAcc {
    let gkey = regr_group_key(t)
    match regr_plan_twin_of(acc, gkey, src) {
        RegrGroupMember as twin => return regr_plan_reuse(acc, twin, key)
        null => { }
    }
    let names = snippet_declared_names(split_snippet_decls_and_stmts(src).decls)
    let idx = regr_group_for_row(acc, gkey, names)
    let fresh = idx == acc.keys.len
    let keys = if fresh { teko::list::push(acc.keys, gkey) } else { acc.keys }
    let specs = if fresh { teko::list::push(acc.specs, RegrGroupSpec { env = t.env; target = t.target }) } else { acc.specs }
    let member = RegrGroupMember { key = key; group = idx; module = $"m{acc.next_module}"; source = src }
    RegrPlanAcc {
        keys = keys; specs = specs
        claims = regr_claims_add(acc.claims, idx, names)
        members = teko::list::push(acc.members, member)
        next_module = acc.next_module + 1
    }
}

/**
 * regr_plan_twin_of — an already-bound member of a group with configuration `gkey` whose snippet
 * source is byte-identical to `source`, or `null` when there is none. Two rows with the same source
 * under the same configuration are the same PROGRAM — they differ only in `args`/`stdin` and in what
 * they expect of it — so they share one module instead of colliding with each other over every name
 * that program declares.
 *
 * @param acc     the accumulator
 * @param gkey    the row's build-configuration key
 * @param source  the row's inline snippet source
 * @return        the member whose module this row can reuse, or null
 */
fn regr_plan_twin_of(acc: RegrPlanAcc, gkey: str, source: str) -> RegrGroupMember | null {
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= acc.members.len { break }
        let mb = acc.members[i]
        if mb.source == source && acc.keys[mb.group] == gkey { return mb }
        i = i + 1
    }
    null
}

/**
 * regr_plan_reuse — bind a row to the module an identical earlier member already contributed. The
 * new member carries NO source, so the group project is written once and the dispatcher gains no
 * duplicate line, while the row still resolves to the right entry at run time.
 *
 * @param acc   the accumulator
 * @param twin  the member whose group and module are reused
 * @param key   the row's scratch-file prefix
 * @return      the accumulator with the row bound to the twin's module
 */
fn regr_plan_reuse(acc: RegrPlanAcc, twin: RegrGroupMember, key: str) -> RegrPlanAcc {
    let member = RegrGroupMember { key = key; group = twin.group; module = twin.module; source = "" }
    RegrPlanAcc {
        keys = acc.keys; specs = acc.specs; claims = acc.claims
        members = teko::list::push(acc.members, member)
        next_module = acc.next_module
    }
}

/**
 * regr_plan_feature — fold every groupable row of ONE feature into the accumulator, walking exactly
 * as the runner does so the row prefixes (`<feature prefix>.<scenario>.<row>`) it records are the
 * same strings the runner later looks up. A COMPILE-FAIL feature is skipped whole, matching
 * `run_one_feature`'s own dispatch: every row of such a feature goes down the compile-fail path,
 * where a successful build would be a failure.
 *
 * @param regr_dir  the directory the `.tkr` lives in (to resolve a `Given source` file)
 * @param feat    the parsed feature
 * @param prefix  the feature's scratch-file prefix
 * @param acc     the accumulator
 * @return        the accumulator with the feature's groupable rows bound
 */
fn regr_plan_feature(regr_dir: str, feat: TkrFeature, prefix: str, acc: RegrPlanAcc) -> RegrPlanAcc {
    if tkr_feature_is_compile_fail(feat) { return acc }
    mut cur = acc
    mut si: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if si >= feat.scenarios.len { break }
        let sc = feat.scenarios[si]
        if tkb_scenario_is_declarative(sc) { si = si + 1; continue }
        let specs = match tkr_expand_outline(sc) { []Tkr as xs => xs; error => return cur }
        mut ri: u64 = 0
        loop {
            if ri >= specs.len { break }
            let t = specs[ri]
            if regr_row_groupable(regr_dir, t) {
                cur = regr_plan_add_row(cur, t, $"{prefix}.{si}.{ri}", regr_row_source(regr_dir, t))
            }
            ri = ri + 1
        }
        si = si + 1
    }
    cur
}

/**
 * regr_plan_file — fold every groupable row of ONE listed `.tkr` file into the accumulator. An
 * unreadable or unparseable file contributes nothing: the ORDINARY run reports that as the manifest
 * error it is (`run_regression_sources`), and planning must never be the thing that reports it.
 *
 * @param path    the listed `.tkr` path
 * @param prefix  the file's scratch-file prefix
 * @param acc     the accumulator
 * @return        the accumulator with the file's groupable rows bound
 */
fn regr_plan_file(path: str, prefix: str, acc: RegrPlanAcc) -> RegrPlanAcc {
    let src = match teko::io::read_file(path) { str as s => s; error => return acc }
    let feats = match parse_tkr(src) { []TkrFeature as xs => xs; error => return acc }
    mut cur = acc
    mut fi: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if fi >= feats.len { break }
        cur = regr_plan_feature(dirname_of(path), feats[fi], $"{prefix}.f{fi}", cur)
        fi = fi + 1
    }
    cur
}

/**
 * regr_group_member_count — how many members the plan assigned to group `g`.
 *
 * @param members  every member binding
 * @param g        the group index
 * @return         that group's member count
 */
fn regr_group_member_count(members: []RegrGroupMember, g: u64) -> u64 {
    mut n: u64 = 0
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= members.len { break }
        if members[i].group == g { n = n + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    n
}

/**
 * regr_plan_groups — the whole plan for a run: walk every listed `.tkr` file in manifest order and
 * bind every groupable row to its configuration's group. The prefixes recorded here are built from
 * the same `<work>/<sanitized path>` stem the run pass uses, which is what lets a row be recognised
 * later without a second identity scheme.
 *
 * @param m     the resolved manifest (its `test_regression_files` is the walk)
 * @param work  the regression scratch work tree
 * @return      the plan (its `members` empty when nothing is groupable)
 */
pub fn regr_plan_groups(m: Manifest, work: str) -> RegrGroupPlan {
    mut acc = regr_plan_empty()
    mut si: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if si >= m.test_regression_files.len { break }
        let path = m.test_regression_files[si]
        acc = regr_plan_file(path, work ~ "/" ~ regr_sanitize(path), acc)
        si = si + 1
    }
    RegrGroupPlan { specs = acc.specs; members = acc.members }
}

/**
 * regr_group_write_project — materialize group `g`'s project on disk: the synthesized `.tkp`, one
 * `src/<module>/body.tks` per CONTRIBUTING member, and the generated `main.tks` dispatcher. A member
 * that reuses an identical earlier member's module carries no source (`RegrGroupMember.source`) and
 * so writes nothing and adds no second dispatcher line. The tree is wiped first, so no module of a
 * previous run sharing this directory can survive into this one (the same hermeticity
 * `compile_snippet_text` gives a scratch snippet project).
 *
 * @param members  every member binding (only those of group `g` are written)
 * @param g        the group index
 * @param dir      the group project directory
 */
fn regr_group_write_project(members: []RegrGroupMember, g: u64, dir: str) {
    match sh_rm_rf(dir) { error => { }; null => { } }
    match mkdir_p(teko::str::concat(dir, "/src/")) { error => { }; null => { } }
    match teko::io::write_file(dir ~ "/teko.tkp", regr_group_manifest_text()) { error => { }; null => { } }
    mut dispatch = ""
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= members.len { break }
        let mb = members[i]
        if mb.group == g && mb.source.len > 0 {
            match mkdir_p(teko::str::concat(dir, "/src/", mb.module, "/")) { error => { }; null => { } }
            match teko::io::write_file(dir ~ "/src/" ~ mb.module ~ "/body.tks", regr_group_module_text(mb.source)) { error => { }; null => { } }
            dispatch = dispatch ~ regr_group_dispatch_line(mb.module)
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    match teko::io::write_file(dir ~ "/main.tks", regr_group_main_text(dispatch)) { error => { }; null => { } }
}

/**
 * regr_group_skipped — the `RegrGroupBuild` of a group that was NOT built (too few members, or its
 * build failed). Its members fall back to their own per-scenario builds, so it costs nothing and
 * claims nothing.
 *
 * @param members  how many rows the group would have covered
 * @param times    what the attempt cost (zero when nothing was attempted)
 * @return         an un-built group outcome
 */
fn regr_group_skipped(members: u64, times: PhaseTimes) -> RegrGroupBuild {
    RegrGroupBuild { ok = false; binp = ""; members = members; times = times }
}

/**
 * regr_group_build_one — generate and build ONE group, reporting the outcome on stdout BEFORE any
 * scenario runs (the ruling's *"executar estes builds ANTES de qualquer teste começar, mostrando a
 * saída deles no início"*). The build is dry (`--no-verify`, via `compile_regressive`) like every
 * other regression build.
 *
 * A group below `REGR_GROUP_MIN_MEMBERS` is not generated at all. A group whose build FAILS reports
 * the compiler's own diagnostic tail and is marked un-built: its members then take the ordinary
 * per-scenario path, so the failure costs speed and never correctness.
 *
 * @param exe      the absolute path to this compiler binary
 * @param plan     the whole plan (its members select this group's)
 * @param g        the group index
 * @param work     the regression scratch work tree
 * @param n        how many groups there are (the report's `g/n`)
 * @return         the group's build outcome
 */
fn regr_group_build_one(exe: str, plan: RegrGroupPlan, g: u64, work: str, n: u64) -> RegrGroupBuild {
    let count = regr_group_member_count(plan.members, g)
    if count < REGR_GROUP_MIN_MEMBERS { return regr_group_skipped(count, phase_zero()) }
    let spec = plan.specs[g]
    let dir = $"{work}/.group{g}"
    let t_start = regr_now_ns()
    regr_group_write_project(plan.members, g, dir)
    let gen_ns = regr_now_ns() - t_start
    let comp = cap_with_extra_harness(compile_regressive(exe, dir, with_regr_cov_env(spec.env, dir ~ ".compile"), dir ~ ".compile"), gen_ns)
    let times = PhaseTimes { harness_ns = comp.harness_ns; compile_ns = comp.child_ns; run_ns = 0; builds = 1 }
    let cfg = regr_group_config_text(spec)
    if comp.exit != 0 {
        teko::io::println($"teko: regression group {g + 1}/{n} FAILED to build ({count} scenario(s), {cfg}) — each falls back to its own build")
        teko::io::println(compile_failure_message(comp))
        return regr_group_skipped(count, times)
    }
    teko::io::println($"teko: regression group {g + 1}/{n} built in {regr_ms_text(phase_total_ns(times))} — {count} scenario(s), {cfg}")
    RegrGroupBuild { ok = true; binp = artifact_path_for_target($"{dir}/bin/{REGR_GROUP_PROJECT_NAME}", spec.target); members = count; times = times }
}

/**
 * regr_group_config_text — a group's configuration rendered for its report line: the target it
 * builds for and the env tokens it builds under. What a reader needs to tell two groups apart.
 *
 * @param spec  the group's configuration
 * @return      the rendered configuration
 */
fn regr_group_config_text(spec: RegrGroupSpec) -> str {
    let target = if spec.target.len == 0 { HOST_TARGET } else { spec.target }
    mut env = ""
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= spec.env.len { break }
        if env.len > 0 { env = env ~ " " }
        env = env ~ spec.env[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    if env.len == 0 { return $"target {target}, no env" }
    $"target {target}, env {env}"
}

/**
 * regr_build_groups — build every planned group, in plan order, printing each outcome as it lands.
 * Runs BEFORE the first scenario, so the log opens with what the run is about to cost and with any
 * build failure already named.
 *
 * @param exe   the absolute path to this compiler binary
 * @param plan  the plan
 * @param work  the regression scratch work tree
 * @return      the grouping the runner consults per row
 */
pub fn regr_build_groups(exe: str, plan: RegrGroupPlan, work: str) -> RegrGroups {
    if plan.members.len == 0 { return regr_groups_empty() }
    mut builds: []RegrGroupBuild = teko::list::empty()
    mut g: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if g >= plan.specs.len { break }
        builds = teko::list::push(builds, regr_group_build_one(exe, plan, g, work, plan.specs.len))
        g = g + 1
    }
    RegrGroups { members = plan.members; builds = builds }
}

/**
 * regr_groups_total — the summed cost of every grouped build, folded into the run's phase total so
 * the summary line still accounts for every build the run paid for.
 *
 * @param gs  the grouping
 * @return    the summed measurement of the group builds
 */
pub fn regr_groups_total(gs: RegrGroups) -> PhaseTimes {
    mut acc = phase_zero()
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= gs.builds.len { break }
        acc = phase_add(acc, gs.builds[i].times)
        i = i + 1
    }
    acc
}

/**
 * regr_group_slot — the grouped artifact and selector for the row whose scratch prefix is `key`, or
 * `null` when the row was not grouped (or its group did not build). The `null` answer is the whole
 * fallback: the caller then resolves the row's artifact exactly as it did before grouping existed.
 *
 * @param gs   the grouping
 * @param key  the row's scratch-file prefix
 * @return     the row's slot, or null when it has none
 */
pub fn regr_group_slot(gs: RegrGroups, key: str) -> RegrGroupSlot | null {
    mut i: u64 = 0
    loop {
        if i >= gs.members.len { break }
        let mb = gs.members[i]
        if mb.key == key {
            let b = gs.builds[mb.group]
            if !b.ok { return null }
            return RegrGroupSlot { binp = b.binp; module = mb.module }
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    null
}

/**
 * regr_with_entry_env — `t` with the group selector (`REGR_GROUP_ENTRY_VAR=<module>`) appended to
 * its child env, so the group binary runs THIS row's snippet. Appended to the row's OWN env rather
 * than replacing it: a `Given env`/`Given backend` token still has to reach the child.
 *
 * @param t       the lowered row
 * @param module  the row's module namespace inside its group
 * @return        `t` with the selector token in its env
 */
pub fn regr_with_entry_env(t: Tkr, module: str) -> Tkr {
    Tkr {
        args = t.args; stdin = t.stdin; kind = t.kind; exit = t.exit; stdout = t.stdout; stderr = t.stderr
        targets = t.targets; expect = t.expect; golden = t.golden; diagnostic = t.diagnostic
        source_inline = t.source_inline; source_file = t.source_file
        env = teko::list::push(t.env, $"{REGR_GROUP_ENTRY_VAR}={module}")
        target = t.target; in_place = t.in_place; dep_dir = t.dep_dir
        check_wellformed = t.check_wellformed; check_module_valid = t.check_module_valid
    }
}
